FILE: app/src/limited-orbit.ts
```ts
import type { ArcRotateCamera, SceneContext } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import { attachControl, setCameraLimits } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function attachLimitedOrbit(
  camera: ArcRotateCamera,
  canvas: HTMLCanvasElement,
  scene: SceneContext,
  minDistance: number,
  maxDistance: number
): () => void {
  const detachControl = attachControl(camera, canvas, scene);
  const stopClamping = setCameraLimits(
    camera,
    { lowerRadiusLimit: minDistance, upperRadiusLimit: maxDistance },
    scene
  );

  return () => {
    detachControl();
    stopClamping();
  };
}
```

I composed the two library calls that do the job: `attachControl` wires up the standard orbit/zoom pointer handlers on the canvas, and `setCameraLimits` clamps the radius to the given range both immediately (so an out-of-range starting pose is pulled in) and on every subsequent radius write (covering wheel zoom via the per-frame offset and pinch which writes radius directly). The returned dispose function tears down both in order so no canvas listeners or per-frame hooks remain. I wasn't unsure of anything — the notes confirm that `setCameraLimits` clamps at the point of each write and zeroes the inertial offset when a bound is hit, which is exactly the "however hard the user scrolls or pinches" guarantee you asked for.
